<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The Prodigal Son parable</h4>
<p>The key is telling it from the father's perspective, not the son's. The center is the father's heart. Help the group notice that the father <strong>runs</strong> (undignified for an older man in that culture), <strong>embraces and kisses</strong> (gestures of tenderness that break through distance), and <strong>throws a feast</strong> (total restoration of the son's dignity).</p>

<h4>The name YHWH</h4>
<p>Explain that for the Jews it was so sacred it was never pronounced. This helps us understand the reverence owed to God. But Jesus invites us to call him &laquo;Abba,&raquo; a closeness that does not remove reverence but transforms it into filial trust.</p>

<h4>&laquo;God is Love&raquo;</h4>
<p>This phrase (1 Jn 4:8) is the heart of Christian revelation. It is not an abstract definition; it is a declaration of identity. God is not primarily power, justice, or wisdom (though he is also these), but love. Everything else is understood from this.</p>

<h4>Filial trust vs. slavery</h4>
<p>Some participants may have a distorted image of God as a severe judge waiting for them to fail. Help them see that the Gospel presents exactly the opposite. Ask: &laquo;Where does this image of a severe God come from?&raquo;</p>

<h4>The problem of evil</h4>
<p>Someone will almost certainly ask about suffering. You don't need a complete answer. Point to the Cross as God's response to evil: not an explanation, but a redeeming presence. God does not eliminate suffering, but assumes it and transforms it.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the metaphor of water and thirst: God is like a dad who gives us water when we are thirsty. Activities: draw the burning bush, color the father embracing the son, memorize &laquo;God is Love&raquo; with hand signs.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their experience of authority: Have they had a father figure who disappointed them? How does that relate to the image of God? Use examples from movies or shows about fathers. The word &laquo;Abba&raquo; can help break the image of a distant God.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults carry wounds from their father figure. Be sensitive: not everyone has a good experience of fatherhood. Help them distinguish between human fatherhood (limited, sometimes failed) and divine fatherhood (perfect, unconditional). Testimony from converts who discovered God as Father can be very powerful.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the Trinity: the Father loves the Son from all eternity, and that love is the Holy Spirit. God's fatherhood is not a metaphor; it is a theological reality. You can quote the Church Fathers (St. Augustine, St. John Chrysostom) on divine fatherhood.</p>

</section>

