<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;If God is an almighty Father, why does he let innocent people suffer?&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is perhaps the hardest question of faith. The Catechism offers no easy answer, but points out that God respects our freedom and that from every evil he can bring forth a greater good. The Cross is the supreme example: the worst crime in history became the greatest salvation. It is not about understanding evil, but trusting that God is present even in suffering (CCC 311-314).</p>

<h4>&laquo;Isn't it sexist to call God &laquo;Father&raquo;? Does God have gender?&raquo;</h4>
<p>God has no biological gender. The Catechism says God is &laquo;neither man nor woman&raquo; (CCC 239). However, Jesus revealed God as &laquo;Father&raquo; and Scripture uses this language. God's fatherhood does not imply gender, but a relationship: he is the origin of all things, the one who gives life, who loves unconditionally. There are also maternal images of God in the Bible (Is 49:15; 66:13), showing that all human fatherhood and motherhood are imperfect reflections of God's love.</p>

<h4>&laquo;What if I had a bad father and I struggle to call God &laquo;Father&raquo;?&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is very understandable. The image of God as Father can be painful for those who have suffered abuse, abandonment, or indifference from their earthly father. In that case, it can help to begin with the image of God as &laquo;Abba&raquo; (a new word, without negative baggage), or to focus on the maternal aspects of God. The key is to remember that divine fatherhood is the measure of all human fatherhood, not the other way around (Eph 3:14-15). God's love heals and transcends all our human experiences.</p>

<h4>&laquo;Isn't the God of the Old Testament more of a severe judge than a loving Father?&raquo;</h4>
<p>It is a common mistake to think the OT shows an angry God and the NT a loving God. In the OT we also find a God full of mercy, who forgives Israel again and again (Hos 11:1-9; Ps 103:8-14). And in the NT there is also judgment (Mt 25:31-46). The Catechism explains that there is a single progressive revelation: the God who creates, chooses, liberates, and forgives in the OT is the same God who reveals himself as Father in Jesus Christ (CCC 238).</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Father, you who are the source of all love,<br />
thank you for calling me to serve you in catechesis.<br />
Give me a father's heart for those who listen to me:<br />
patience when they do not understand,<br />
tenderness when they doubt,<br />
firmness when they stray.<br />
May I not be an obstacle to their knowing you,<br />
but a reflection of your loving fatherhood.<br />
May seeing me help them glimpse who you are.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

